WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.485
Counting gives probabilities only after we identify equally likely outcomes

00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:08.162
Two independent fair dice produce thirty-six ordered face pairs

00:00:08.162 --> 00:00:17.967
The first die and second die have separate positions, so a two followed by a five differs from a five followed by a two.

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:26.557
Six ordered pairs add to seven: one and six, two and five, three and four, and their reversals

00:00:26.557 --> 00:00:31.012
Divide those six favorable outcomes by the thirty-six equally likely pairs

00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:35.467
We count the underlying outcomes before grouping them by their sum.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:40.090
The possible sums themselves are not equally likely

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.244
Only one ordered pair produces two, while six produce seven

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:52.967
This histogram shows how many pairs belong to each sum, preventing the mistake of assigning every possible sum the same probability.

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:56.356
Without replacement, later counts change

00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:04.715
From four marked objects and two plain objects, the probability of two marked draws is four sixths times three fifths

00:01:04.715 --> 00:01:10.567
After the first marked object stays out, only three marked objects remain among five.
